<!-- cell: title-page id="f398250d-d032-4e2d-9574-43256f0252b8" title="The Weather in the Book" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="31980ebb-d5a9-4e37-b24c-af8d094ec69c" -->

Wren had been apprenticed to Master Corvin for three years, and in three years he had taught her to sweep the tower stairs, grind salt for the wards, and copy out the names of clouds in a neat hand. He had not taught her a single spell.

The tower stood alone on the Saltings, where the marsh met the grey sea, and from its top window Wren could see the village of Ebb two miles inland. That summer there had been no rain in Ebb for nine weeks. The wells were mud. The barley stood white in the fields.

"Can't you do something?" she asked Corvin.

"Weather is not a thing you do," he said, without looking up from his book. "It is a thing you ask, and then you are responsible for the answer."

That night, while he slept, Wren took the Book of Weathers from its chain. She knew where the key was. She had swept around it a thousand times.

She found the rain-calling on the forty-first page and read it aloud from the top of the tower, every word, the way she had copied the names of clouds. She felt the words leave her like birds.

The clouds came in from the sea fast and black. The rain fell on Ebb, and Wren laughed out loud. Then it fell harder. Then the marsh began to rise around the tower, and the river below the village burst its banks, and she saw the lights of Ebb going out one by one under the water.

She tried to unsay the spell. The book did not have an unsaying.

She ran down the stairs she had swept so many times and woke her master.

Corvin listened without a word, pulled his coat on over his nightshirt, and climbed to the top window. He looked at the drowning fields for a long moment.

"I can't stop it for you," he said. "It answers the one who asked. But I can tell you the words, if you will say them."

So he spoke the long, old words of thanks and release, one at a time, and Wren repeated them into the storm with salt water streaming down her face. The last word was the hardest. It meant, as near as anyone could translate it, enough.

The rain thinned to drizzle, then to mist. By morning the river had found its banks again, and the wells of Ebb were full.

Corvin made two cups of tea and set one in front of her. "Lesson one," he said. "Tomorrow we begin with clouds that only threaten."

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="cd598448-ac79-4c36-8f08-e69c2a1908f3" title="Wren" words="0-38, 257-262, 270-284" -->

An impatient apprentice who, after three years of sweeping, steals her master's book to call rain.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="939043af-bb7c-4d22-92c2-a0890da3411a" title="Master Corvin" words="106-134, 285-302, 408-420" -->

The old weather-wizard who teaches that weather is asked, not done, and helps Wren undo her spell only by having her say the words herself.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="f1f8d88e-a7a2-4ecc-befd-fde22adb376c" title="The tower on the Saltings" words="47-75, 224-256" -->

The lone tower on the salt marsh by the sea, two miles from the drought-struck village of Ebb.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="91b45dcb-cbc6-41a4-8b1e-bc2721c1c832" title="Ambition" words="39-46, 135-148, 210-219" -->

Wren's wish to do real magic before she is ready.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="5d3e972e-1223-454d-9e7d-168877295c67" title="Humility" words="313-338, 368-383, 421-432" -->

What Wren learns: to ask for help, to take responsibility, and to say 'enough'.

<!-- cell: plot id="412f20e5-ff34-486f-b877-2d87d0758666" title="The rain-calling" words="76-87, 135-148, 163-191, 200-256, 257-269, 270-284, 313-338, 339-367, 384-407" -->

During a drought Wren steals the Book of Weathers and calls rain; it floods the village; she wakes Corvin, who gives her the words of release to say herself; the flood recedes and her real lessons begin.
